he grows (}{\i\f211 cultivates}{\f208 ) strawberries, apples and various vegetables in the garden\par 2.\emdash  The job of a gardener (}{\i\f211 cultivator of a garden}{\f208 ) can be-called horticulture (}{\i\f211 cultivation of the garden}{\f208 ).\par 3.\emdash  Similarly, cultivators of the field, or farmers, grow (}{\i\f211 cultivate}{\f208 ) grain in the fields.\par 4.\emdash  Give wine to the cultivator of the field (}{\i\f211 farmer}{\f208 ).\par 5.\emdash  Give beer to the gardeners.\par 6.\emdash  Where is the house of the happy farmers?\par 7.\emdash  [Farmers say that] one swallow (}{\i\f211 is said by farmers}{\f208 ) doesn\rquote t make (}{\i\f211 not to make}{\f208 ) a summer (}{\i\f211 spring}{\f208 ).\par \par And now let us try to clear up the mysteries of the 3}{\f208\super rd}{\f208  declension. It is very important, because it is the one that corresponds to the most general and most varied forms.\par \par Let us take for instance }{\b\f210 c}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 ltor f}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 lix}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 the happy gardener}{\f208 :\par \par }{\i\f211 \tab \tab \tab Singular:\tab \tab \tab Plural:\par }{\f208 nom.\tab \tab \tab CULTOR FELIX\tab \tab CULTOR}{\b\f210 es}{\f208  FELIC}{\b\f210 es}{\f208 \par acc.\tab \tab \tab CULTOR}{\b\f210 em}{\f208  FELIC}{\b\f210 em}{\f208 \tab CULTOR}{\b\f210 es}{\f208  FELIC}{\b\f210 es}{\f208 \par gen.\tab \tab \tab CULTOR}{\b\f210 is}{\f208  FELIC}{\b\f210 is}{\f208 \tab \tab CULTOR}{\b\f210 um}{\f208  FELIC}{\b\f210 ium}{\f208 \par dat.\tab \tab \tab CULTOR}{\b\f210 i}{\f208  FELIC}{\b\f210 i}{\f208 \tab \tab CULTOR}{\b\f210 ibus}{\f208  FELIC}{\b\f210 ibus}{\f208 \par abl.\tab \tab \tab CULTOR}{\b\f210 e}{\f208  FELIC}{\b\f210 i}{\f208 \tab \tab CULTOR}{\b\f210 ibus}{\f208  FELIC}{\b\f210 ibus}{\f208 \par \par The difficulties of the third declension are:\par \par 1}{\f208\super st}{\f208  The change of form, sometimes important, which takes place between the nominative and the other cases. The }{\b\f210 nominative is often a shortened from}{\f208 , and sometimes even very shortened. E.g.: i}{\b\f210 ter}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 it}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 neris}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 the way}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 the route}{\f208 . Of course, in the \ldblquote dictionary\rdblquote  presentation, it is the genitive which indicates to us the form of the other cases.\par \par 2}{\f208\super nd}{\f208  The fact that the ablative singular is sometimes in -}{\b\f210 i}{\f208  instead of -}{\b\f210 e}{\f208  and the genitive plural in -}{\b\f210 ium}{\f208  instead of -}{\b\f210 um}{\f208 . We shall return to this point.\par \par The 3}{\f208\super rd}{\f208  declension consists of masculine nouns like }{\b\f210 c}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 ltor}{\f208 , feminine nouns like }{\b\f210 s}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 ror}{\f208 , -o}{\b\f210 ris}{\f208 ; }{\b\f210 m}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 ter}{\f208 , -}{\b\f210 tris}{\f208  (vid. L 4), or ae}{\b\f210 des}{\f208 , -}{\b\f210 is}{\f208 , that we have just seen (P. 5 and N. 6).\par \par It also includes neuters, which differ from the preceding by an accusative which is the same as the nominative, both cases being in -}{\b\f210 a}{\f208  or -}{\b\f210 ia}{\f208  in the plural.\par \par E.g.: }{\b\f210 st}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 rcus}{\f208  (N. 4), acc. }{\b\f210 st}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 rcus}{\f208  (and not }{\i\f211 stercorem}{\f208 !), nom. and acc. pl. }{\b\f210 st}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 rcora}{\f208 . We have already seen }{\b\f210 c}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 rpus}{\f208  (L. 20, P. 2); its genitive singular is }{\b\f210 c}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 rporis}{\f208 ; nominative and acc. pl. }{\b\f210 c}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 rpora}{\f208 .\par \par It will be noted that the majority of these words have at least a syllable less in the nominative than in the other cases. They are called }{\b\i\f212 imparisyllabic}{\f208  (i}{\b\f210 mpar}{\f208 , i}{\b\f210 mparis}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 unequal}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 odd}{\f208 ). On the contrary, the words like ae}{\b\f210 des}{\f208 , ae}{\b\f210 dis}{\f208 , which keep the same number of syllables in all cases (other than the gen., dat., and abl. pl.), are said to be }{\b\i\f212 parisyllabic}{\f208  (}{\b\f210 par}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 p}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 ris}{\f208 , means }{\i\f211 equal}{\f208  or }{\i\f211 even}{\f208 ). The declension of the parisyllabics introduces little peculiarities whic}{\f208 h we shall discuss again later.}{\par }}